            <ArticleTitle>Type B aortic dissection in a patient with unknown left ventricular non-compaction cardiomyopathy: cardiovascular magnetic resonance diagnosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e49-50</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ijcard.2008.11.097</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 61-year-old man was referred at our hospital for a cardiac evaluation after an episode of oppressive chest pain at rest. He was discharged with a MRI diagnosis of non-compaction cardiomyopathy presented with typical angina, left ventricle dysfunction and ischemic EKG findings without atherosclerotic narrowing and also with a type B aortic dissection, documented also by computed tomography.</AbstractText>
